<pr_description>
Consider the following PR description:
Chunked decoder treats 0-length chunk as body continuation instead of end-of-chunks

Problem
I noticed that when decoding chunked responses, the state machine treats a zero-length chunk (the terminating "0\r\n") as if there is more body data instead of signalling the end of the chunked stream. That leads to various decode errors when the decoder then tries to parse trailers or the next chunk size.

Reproduction (minimal example)
Feed a normal chunked response that ends with the terminating zero chunk:

```
let bytes = b"4\r\nWiki\r\n0\r\n\r\n";
// feed `bytes` into the h1 decoder/reader
// (using the local hyper proto/h1 decoder)
let result = decode_chunked(bytes);
println!("{:?}", result);
```

Actual output (what I'm seeing)
Instead of successfully completing the chunked decode, the decoder errors. Example messages observed while debugging:

```
decode: Custom { kind: UnexpectedEof, error: "unexpected EOF during chunk size line" }
called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: Custom { kind: InvalidInput, error: HeaderName }
decode: Custom { kind: InvalidInput, error: "Invalid trailer end LF" }
```

Expected behavior
The terminating "0\r\n" should transition the chunk parser into the end-of-chunks state so that optional trailers can be read (or an empty trailer set returned). Decoding the example above should succeed and indicate end-of-body (and yield any trailers, if present), not produce UnexpectedEof / InvalidInput errors.

Notes
The incorrect behavior appears to be in the chunk-size-line '\n' handling in src/proto/h1/decode.rs — the branch that checks whether size == 0 seems to be doing the opposite of what's intended, so a zero-length chunk ends up being handled as if it were a non-zero chunk. This causes subsequent trailer/header parsing to fail with the errors shown above.
